n? Actually if we remove a node, its children are retained? No, we only remove the node itself; its children must already be removed (since we only consider nodes with no retained children). So the children are already gone. So the only node being removed is this one. Since its symbol is not required, symbol representation doesn't change. So no need to check further? Wait: What if a required symbol is present in a node that is a descendant that is already removed? That symbol is already gone. But required symbols must be present in retained nodes. So if we remove a node whose symbol is not required, it doesn't affect required symbols. So we don't need to check again. However, there is a nuance: The rule \"All required symbols remain represented\" might refer to after removal, the tree (the remaining nodes) must still contain each required symbol at least once. Since we only remove a node that is not required, and its children are already removed (so they were either removed or no longer present), the symbols that were present only in this node's subtree are already gone. So the check is essentially: before removing any node, we must ensure that the required symbols are present in the current retained set. But initially they are present. As we remove nodes, we might remove nodes that are the only carrier of a required symbol? But we don't remove nodes with required symbol. So that's fine. But what about a required symbol that appears in multiple nodes? Removing one non-required node doesn't affect that. However, consider a required symbol that appears in a node that is a parent, and we are about to remove a child that has a different symbol. That doesn't affect required. So maybe no check is needed beyond not removing required symbols. But the instruction says \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" That suggests we should still check. Possibly there are scenarios where removing a node could remove a required symbol indirectly because the required symbol is present only in a descendant that is not yet removed? But we only remove nodes with no retained children, so descendants are gone. So the only place that required symbol could be is in the node itself or its ancestors. If it's in the node itself, we already prevent removal because symbol is required. If it's in an ancestor, still fine. So likely no additional check. But to be safe and follow the contract verbatim, we can check: after removal, is there at least one node (in retained set excluding this node) for each required symbol? Since we already ensure node.symbol not in required_symbols, the check will pass. But it's harmless.\n\n- If conditions satisfied, mark node as removed: add node.id to removed list (in order), remove from retained set.\n\nAfter processing all nodes in the order, we have removed_node_ids in removal order, and retained_node_ids sorted (we need to sort them). The sorting order: \"sorted retained IDs\" - I'll sort ascending lexicographically.\n\nNow, we need to implement this in Python and encode as JSON string under \"implementation\".\n\nEdge cases: The case may have nodes with no children (leaf). That's fine.\n\nImportant: The algorithm must be deterministic. Use exact order: depth descending, then node id ascending.\n\nNote: \"Consider deeper parse-tree nodes before shallower nodes.\" That implies depth descending.\n\nAlso, \"Use node ID as the stable tie break at equal depth.\" So for same depth, we need a fixed order. Node IDs are strings like \"n1\", \"n2\", \"root\". Lexicographic ascending is a natural order.\n\nTest on the first fixture to see if it makes sense. But we don't have expected output. Let's manually simulate first case:\n\nNodes:\nroot (d0, symbol root, removable false)\nn1 (d1, parent root, s1, removable false) - symbol s1 is required\nn2 (d1, parent root, s2, removable true)\nn3 (d1, parent root, s0, removable false)\nn4 (d2, parent n2, s1, removable true)\nn5 (d2, parent n3, s2, removable false)\nn6 (d2, parent n1, s0, removable true)\n\nRequired symbols: root, s1.\n\nStep: sort by depth descending, then id ascending.\nDepth 2: n4, n5, n6 (id order: n4, n5, n6)\nDepth 1: n1, n2, n3\nDepth 0: root\n\nProcess n4: removable true, not root, symbol s1 but s1 is required -> cannot remove, skip.\nProcess n5: removable false -> skip.\nProcess n6: removable true, symbol s0 not required, children? none (leaf). Check required symbols: removing n6 (s0) doesn't affect required symbols (root and s1). So remove n6. Now retained: root,n1,n2,n3,n4,n5 (n6 removed)\nProcess n1: depth1, removable false -> skip.\nProcess n2: depth1, removable true, symbol s2 not required, children? n4 is still retained? n4 is retained (since not removed), so n2 has retained child n4. So cannot remove because node with retained children. Skip.\nProcess n3: depth1, removable false -> skip.\nProcess root: depth0, removable false -> skip.\n\nNow after processing depth1 and depth0, no more changes. Did we miss n4? n4 is at depth2 but already processed. Since n4 is still retained, and n2 cannot be removed. Could n4 be removed later? But we process depth descending and only once each node. In typical iterative reduction, after removing n6, we might need to re-evaluate nodes because their children status changed? But the algorithm as described processes each node once in order. That might not capture the full reduction. For example, n2 has child n4 that is removable. If later n4 is removed, then n2 would have no retained children and could be removed. But we only iterate once. The instruction doesn't say to iterate until no change. It says \"Consider deeper parse-tree nodes before shallower nodes.\" That implies a single pass? But typical Perses algorithm iterates until no removal possible. However, the phrase \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" suggests a greedy single-pass? But then we might miss removals that become possible after removing descendants. But the rule \"never remove a node with retained children\" means we need to remove children first. So a single pass in depth order would handle that: we remove leaves first, then parents. However, in the example above, n4 is at depth2 and is removable, but it has symbol s1 which is required, so it cannot be removed. So n2's child n4 remains. That's fine. If n4 had been removable and not required, then we would have removed n4 first, then later n2 would have no retained children, and then n2 could be removed. But in a single pass, after processing n4 and removing it, we then later process n2 (since n2 is depth1, processed after all depth2 nodes). That would work. So a single pass in descending depth ensures that children are processed before parents. So the example with n6 removal works: n6 removed, then later n1 (parent of n6) still has child n6? No, n6 is removed, so n1 has no retained children? n1's children initially: n6 only. After removing n6, n1 has no retained children. But n1 is not removable (removable false). So it's fine.\n\nNow consider n4: it is required symbol, so stays. n2 remains with retained child n4. That's correct.\n\nWhat about n5? n3 has child n5, n5 not removable, so n3 stays.\n\nThus final removed: only n6. retained: root, n1, n2, n3, n4, n5.\n\nBut is there any other removable node? n4 is removable but required symbol, so cannot. So that seems correct.\n\nNow, we should test second fixture case quickly: similar structure, but utilities differ. The algorithm doesn't consider utility, only removable flag and required symbols. So output will be similar. However, the problem mentions \"utility\" in the nodes but doesn't use it. That's fine.\n\nThus implementation is straightforward.\n\nNow produce the JSON response. We need to write Python source as a JSON string. Use triple quotes or escape newlines. We'll write a define_solve function.\n\nImplementation details:\n- def solve(case):\n    nodes = case[\"nodes\"]\n    required_symbols = case[\"required_symbols\"]\n    node_by_id = {n[\"id\"]: n for n in nodes}\n    children = {n[\"id\"]: [] for n in nodes}\n    for n in nodes:\n        pid = n[\"parent\"]\n        if pid:\n            children[pid].append(n[\"id\"])\n    retained = set(n[\"id\"] for n in nodes)\n    removed = []\n    # Sort nodes by depth descending, then id ascending\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: (-n[\"depth\"], n[\"id\"]))\n    for node in sorted_nodes:\n        nid = node[\"id\"]\n        if nid not in retained:\n            continue\n        if not node[\"removable\"]:\n            continue\n        if nid == \"root\":  # but root could be different id? Usually it's \"root\". But we can check parent null.\n            continue\n        # Also don't remove required symbol\n        if node[\"symbol\"] in required_symbols:\n            continue\n        # Check if any child retained\n        child_ids = children.get(nid, [])\n        if any(cid in retained for cid in child_ids):\n            continue\n        # Check required symbols still represented after removal (optional but safe)\n        # Compute symbols in retained except this node\n        # Since we know node.symbol not required, we can just check that all required symbols exist somewhere else\n        # But if all required symbols are accounted for, proceed\n        # We'll do a quick check:\n        syms_after = set()\n        for rid in retained:\n            if rid == nid:\n                continue\n            syms_after.add(node_by_id[rid][\"symbol\"])\n        if not required_symbols.issubset(syms_after):\n            continue\n        # Remove\n        retained.remove(nid)\n        removed.append(nid)\n    # Sort retained IDs\n    retained_sorted = sorted(retained)\n    return {\"removed_node_ids\": removed, \"retained_node_ids\": retained_sorted}\n\nWe need to ensure we don't mutate case. Already we only read.\n\nNow produce the JSON.\n\nImportant: The output must be exactly one JSON object with key \"implementation\". The value is a string containing the Python source code. We need to escape newlines, backslashes, quotes. Use json.dumps on the string. But we are writing the response as JSON. So we can manually construct the string.\n\nBetter to embed the Python code as a string with proper escaping.\n\nWe'll write the code as a multi-line string, then use json.dumps to convert to a JSON string.\n\nWe need to be careful about indentation.\n\nLet's write the code:\n\n```python\ndef solve(case):\n    nodes = case[\"nodes\"]\n    required_symbols = set(case[\"required_symbols\"])\n    node_by_id = {n[\"id\"]: n for n in nodes}\n    children = {n[\"id\"]: [] for n in nodes}\n    for n in nodes:\n        p = n.get(\"parent\")\n        if p:\n            children[p].append(n[\"id\"])\n    retained = {n[\"id\"] for n in nodes}\n    removed = []\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: (-n[\"depth\"], n[\"id\"]))\n    for node in sorted_nodes:\n        nid = node[\"id\"]\n        if nid not in retained:\n            continue\n        if not node[\"removable\"]:\n            continue\n        # Never remove root (parent is None)\n        if node[\"parent\"] is None:\n            continue\n        if node[\"symbol\"] in required_symbols:\n            continue\n        if any(cid in retained for cid in children.get(nid, [])):\n            continue\n        # Check required symbols preserved\n        # Build symbol set from retained except this node\n        syms = set()\n        for rid in retained:\n            if rid == nid:\n                continue\n            syms.add(node_by_id[rid][\"symbol\"])\n        if not required_symbols.issubset(syms):\n            continue\n        retained.remove(nid)\n        removed.append(nid)\n    return {\n        \"removed_node_ids\": removed,\n        \"retained_node_ids\": sorted(retained)\n    }\n```\n\nNow, we need to output as JSON string with proper escaping. We'll create the implementation string, then wrap in {\"implementation\": \"...\"}. We'll use Python's json.dumps on the entire object.\n\nI'll do it in my mind:\n\n{\"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    nodes = case[\\\"nodes\\\"]\\n    required_symbols = set(case[\\\"required_symbols\\\"])\\n    node_by_id = {n[\\\"id\\\"]: n for n in nodes}\\n    children = {n[\\\"id\\\"]: [] for n in nodes}\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        p = n.get(\\\"parent\\\")\\n        if p:\\n            children[p].append(n[\\\"id\\\"])\\n    retained = {n[\\\"id\\\"] for n in nodes}\\n    removed = []\\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: (-n[\\\"depth\\\"], n[\\\"id\\\"]))\\n    for node in sorted_nodes:\\n        nid = node[\\\"id\\\"]\\n        if nid not in retained:\\n            continue\\n        if not node[\\\"removable\\\"]:\\n            continue\\n        if node[\\\"parent\\\"] is None:\\n            continue\\n        if node[\\\"symbol\\\"] in required_symbols:\\n            continue\\n        if any(cid in retained for cid in children.get(nid, [])):\\n            continue\\n        syms = set()\\n        for rid in retained:\\n            if rid == nid:\\n                continue\\n            syms.add(node_by_id[rid][\\\"symbol\\\"])\\n        if not required_symbols.issubset(syms):\\n            continue\\n        retained.remove(nid)\\n        removed.append(nid)\\n    return {\\n        \\\"removed_node_ids\\\": removed,\\n        \\\"retained_node_ids\\\": sorted(retained)\\n    }\"}\n\nBut we need to ensure all quotes are escaped. In the string, we have double quotes inside, so they must be escaped as \\\". Also newlines as \\n.\n\nLet me write it properly.\n\nWe'll output the final JSON as a string.\n\nI'll produce the final response."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":2088,"completion_tokens":5369,"total_tokens":7457,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":2048},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":4998},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":2048,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens